;\n\nfunction formatCell([x, y]: Cell): string {\n  return `${x},${y}`;\n}\n\nfunction parseCell(key: string): Cell {\n  const [x, y] = key.split(\",\").map(Number);\n  return [x, y];\n}\n\nexport function nextGeneration(liveCells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const liveKeys = new Set(liveCells.map(formatCell));\n  const neighborCountsByKey = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  for (const [x, y] of liveCells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const neighborKey = formatCell([x + dx, y + dy]);\n      neighborCountsByKey.set(\n        neighborKey,\n        (neighborCountsByKey.get(neighborKey) || 0) + 1,\n      );\n    }\n  }\n\n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of neighborCountsByKey) {\n    const isLive = liveKeys.has(key);\n    const willBeAlive = count === 3 || (count === 2 && isLive);\n    if (willBeAlive) {\n      next.push(parseCell(key));\n    }\n  }\n\n  return next;\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784971709672},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now produce final report. Need mention tests passing, mass calculations, naming evaluation. Could also mention Rule 2 trump APP.\n\nLet's craft a concise markdown final.\n\nPotential mass table:\nCurrent (before) mass:\nConstants 22, Bindings 15, Invocations 24, Conditionals 8, Loops 15, Assignments 12 => 96.\nRefactored mass:\nConstants 22, Bindings 17, Invocations 24, Conditionals 8, Loops 15, Assignments 12 => 98.\nMass change +2.\n\nBut we need explain counting method. Maybe include note that offsets constant literals are counted. If they want to exclude, same delta.\n\nLet's ensure the invocations count: After parseCell, formatCell (2), map(Number), split, set, get, has, push, new Set, new Map, liveCells.map. That's 12. Good.\n\nBindings after: formatCell x,y (2); parseCell key, x,y (3); nextGeneration liveCells, liveKeys, neighborCountsByKey, next, x,y,dx,dy,neighborKey,key,count,isLive,willBeAlive (12) =17.\n\nBefore: cellToKey cell (1); keyToCell k